{"book":{"id":"book-women-who-woke-up-the-law-inside-the-cases-that-changed-women-s-rights-in-canada-karin-wells","slug":"women-who-woke-up-the-law-inside-the-cases-that-changed-women-s-rights-in-canada-karin-wells","title":"Women Who Woke up the Law: Inside the Cases that Changed Women’s Rights in Canada","authors":[{"id":"person-karin-wells","name":"Karin Wells"}],"isbn13":[],"subjects":["Gender & Sexuality"],"topics":["Reproductive Rights & Family Policy","Gender & Feminism","Human Rights & International Law","Intellectual History & Ideas"],"centralFigures":[],"links":{"amazon":"https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Women%20Who%20Woke%20up%20the%20Law%3A%20Inside%20the%20Cases%20that%20Changed%20Women%E2%80%99s%20Rights%20in%20Canada%20Karin%20Wells","bookshop":"https://bookshop.org/search?keywords=Women%20Who%20Woke%20up%20the%20Law%3A%20Inside%20the%20Cases%20that%20Changed%20Women%E2%80%99s%20Rights%20in%20Canada%20Karin%20Wells","indiebound":"https://www.indiebound.org/search/book?keys=Women%20Who%20Woke%20up%20the%20Law%3A%20Inside%20the%20Cases%20that%20Changed%20Women%E2%80%99s%20Rights%20in%20Canada%20Karin%20Wells","worldcat":"https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=Women%20Who%20Woke%20up%20the%20Law%3A%20Inside%20the%20Cases%20that%20Changed%20Women%E2%80%99s%20Rights%20in%20Canada%20Karin%20Wells"},"sourceIds":["source-award-record-shaughnessy-cohen-political-writing-wikipedia-shaughnessy-cohen-prize-for-political-writing","source-apple-books-summary-women-who-woke-up-the-law-inside-the-cases-that-changed-women-s-rights-in-canada-karin-wells"],"readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"renownProfile":{"knowsBook":false,"publicFame":8,"criticalRenown":18,"controversy":5,"llmAffinity":78,"affinityResidual":8.76,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.15,"criticalRenown":0.15,"controversy":0.15,"llmAffinity":0.86},"tags":{"craft":"narrative-drive","evidence":"reportage","stance":"revisionism"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"summary":"“Who was the woman trying to convince a jury in a tiny courthouse in Nova Scotia that it was self-defense when she killed her partner; and who was the young woman walking into the palais de justice in small-town Quebec arguing that it was her choice, not his, to have an abortion? What was it that pushed these women on, even when the lawyers said it was hopeless?” From the award-winning author of The Abortion Caravan and More Than a Footnote, Karin Wells once again pulls us into the lives—and this time, the legal trials—of a group of women integral to the advancement of women’s rights in Canada. Eliza Campbell, Chantale Daigle, Jeannette Corbiere Lavell—these Women Who Woke Up the Law often had no idea what they were facing in the courts, or the price they would have to pay. Some never saw justice themselves, but they left a legal legacy. Their bold determination is something we need now more than ever to guard the hard-won gains in women’s rights.","displaySummary":"What was it that pushed these women on, even when the lawyers said it was hopeless?” From the award-winning author of The Abortion Caravan and More Than a Footnote, Karin Wells once again pulls us into the lives—and this time, the legal trials—of a group of women integral to the advancement of women’s rights in Canada. Eliza Campbell, Chantale Daigle, Jeannette Corbiere Lavell—these Women Who Woke Up the Law often had no idea what they were facing in the courts, or the price they would have to pay. Some never saw justice themselves, but they left a legal legacy. Their bold determination is something we need now more than ever to guard the hard-won gains in women’s rights.","primarySubject":"Gender & Sexuality","primaryTopic":"Reproductive Rights & Family Policy","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Gender & Sexuality","confidence":"low","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Gender & Sexuality","score":5